{"id": "sevt_01Hu73Lkzj8HNGBNJ5Yv9qN1", "content": [{"text": "**Finding:** `49333d6` (\"tidy order summary helpers\", alice, deployed 14:15:40Z, still live) replaced a batched order-items query with one query per open order. Checkout p99 starts climbing at 14:16 and the DB pool hits 100% by 14:46.\n\n**Deploy vs. metric timing (checkout)**\n- **14:03:30 `dcaf6a6`** (inline order item fetch) \u2192 p99 jumps to ~2.7\u20134.1 s at 14:03. Rolled back 14:07, and p99 drops to ~62 ms at 14:07. The same symptom, reverted quickly.\n- **14:15:40 `49333d6`** \u2192 p99 starts rising at 14:16 (125 ms) and climbs steadily to ~420 ms by 14:37. No rollback, so the ramp continues.\n- **14:33:10 `9ea808a`** (structured request logging) \u2192 rolled back at 14:35:30. Its diff only changes log call formatting, and nothing in it touches the DB path, so it doesn't explain the ramp.\n- **14:38 step** \u2192 p99 jumps from 414 ms to 3,757 ms with no deploy at that time. It coincides with DB pool utilization passing ~74% and climbing to 100% by 14:46. My read is that pool saturation is what turned the slow ramp into the step, though the logs don't prove that directly.\n\n**Diff for `49333d6`:** the old code ran one `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id__in=ids)` and grouped in memory. The new code loops over `rows` and issues `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id=order.order_id)` for each one.\n\n**Log confirmation (`app.log`)**\n- 3,312 log lines match `FROM order_items WHERE order_id = ?`, all from checkout. This is the per-row form; the batched form is `WHERE order_id IN (?)`.\n- Per-row query volume tracks the latency: it is elevated at 14:03\u201314:06 (the rolled-back `dcaf6a6` window), then rises from ~14:16 and reaches 100\u2013170 per minute by 14:38\u201314:59.\n- At peak, one request ran 16 order-item queries.\n\n**Recommended next steps**\n- Roll back `49333d6` (or restore the batched `order_id__in` query). Expect p99 to fall to baseline within about a minute, as it did after the 14:07 rollback.\n- Add a query-count check for checkout in CI to catch N+1 patterns before deploy.\n- Check whether the 14:38 pool saturation caused any checkout errors. `error_rate` was non-zero from 14:46 (up to ~0.4%), and it started after the pool hit saturation.\n\nConfidence: high for `49333d6` as the source of the ramp, based on the diff, the query logs, and the timing. Medium for the 14:38 step being pool-driven, since I have no pool-wait or connection-timeout data.\n\n**Root cause:** `49333d6` \u2014 switching order-item loading from one batched query to one query per open order multiplied DB round-trips per checkout request, which exhausted the checkout DB pool and drove p99 from ~75 ms to over 4 s.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T11:50:22.680103Z", "type": "agent.message"}
